{"id": "live-386", "title": "Rubicon Real Estate Holdings, LLC v. City of Pontiac, Michigan (Supreme Court Ends Developer's Permitting-Delay Suit Over Collapsed Cannabis Project)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-11-09", "jurisdiction": "Supreme Court of the United States", "state": "MI", "amount": "$60 million sought", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/110926zor_ap6c.pdf", "summary": "The U.S. Supreme Court, in its November 9, 2026 order list, denied certiorari in Rubicon Real Estate Holdings, LLC v. City of Pontiac, Michigan, leaving intact a published Sixth Circuit opinion that affirmed summary judgment for the city. Rubicon had sued Pontiac and its clerk in January 2023, alleging nearly four years of permitting delay on a planned $45 million medical-marijuana cultivation and processing redevelopment of a roughly 26.5-acre site caused its construction lender to withdraw financing, and seeking about $60 million in damages under Section 1983 and related constitutional theories.", "significance": "The denial closes out, at the highest level, a recurring fact pattern for cannabis and other use-restricted commercial redevelopment: courts treat ordinary municipal permitting delay, even when it is severe enough to collapse a project's financing, as discretionary bureaucratic process rather than a federal civil-rights violation absent concrete evidence of discriminatory or retaliatory intent. It is a signal to developers and lenders on politically sensitive land uses to price permitting-timeline risk into deal structures rather than count on a later constitutional claim to shift that risk back onto the municipality.", "judge": null, "tags": ["zoning", "cannabis", "michigan", "section-1983", "scotus"]}
